{"revision_id":94,"created":"2008-10-21T00:00:00+00:00","metadata":{"publication_info":[{"cnum":"C08-07-30","parent_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1256391"},"conference_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/conferences/978318"}}],"citation_count_without_self_citations":56,"citation_count":57,"core":true,"titles":[{"title":"The SNO+ Experiment"},{"title":"The SNO+ Experiment","source":"arXiv"}],"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","authors":[{"uuid":"ae6664ce-f990-4031-aa73-a6a401836379","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1013865"},"full_name":"Chen, Mark C.","signature_block":"CANm","curated_relation":true}],"curated":true,"license":[{"url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","license":"arXiv nonexclusive-distrib 1.0","material":"preprint"}],"texkeys":["Chen:2008un"],"citeable":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"The SNO+ experiment is the follow-up to the Sudbury Neutrino Observatory (SNO). The heavy water that was in SNO will be replaced with a liquid scintillator of linear alkylbenzene (plus fluor). SNO+ has many physics goals including detecting pep and CNO solar neutrinos, detecting geo-neutrinos, studying reactor neutrino oscillations, serving as a supernova neutrino detector and carrying out a search for neutrinoless double beta decay by adding neodymium to the liquid scintillator. Since a large amount of 150Nd isotope can be added to SNO+, a competitive search would be possible, with sensitivity below 100 meV using natural Nd and sensitivity below 40 meV with enriched neodymium.","source":"arXiv"}],"references":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/608692"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"1","page_start":"1","journal_title":"Ann.Rev.Nucl.Part.Sci.","journal_volume":"52"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/647907"},"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"hep-ph/0404088","publication_info":{"artid":"198","page_sta